<h2>The deception</h2>
<p>The first plaintiff, a cardiac surgeon of national repute and founding chairman of a listed hospital chain, discovered Facebook pages and YouTube channels running videos in which his face and voice — synthesised with AI and deepfake tools — endorsed drugs claimed to cure diabetes and hypertension, and dispensed health advice he had never given. Some videos carried the hospital company&rsquo;s registered device mark and branded hashtags. The deception was working: a member of the public wrote to the hospital in November 2024 asking whether a video was genuine.</p>

<h2>The procedural path to decree</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>November 2024</strong> — ex parte ad interim injunction granted; directions issued to the platforms and government defendants for takedown and blocking.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Compliance</strong> — the interim directions were complied with; one defendant undertook not to upload the impugned videos and was held to it.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Default</strong> — the content-posting defendants filed no written statements and their right was closed; no defendant contested the suit.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>October 2026</strong> — the suit was decreed on the pleadings, the interim order made absolute, and a permanent injunction passed.</div>
</div>

<h2>Decree without trial — and its discipline</h2>
<p>The Court did not treat the defendants&rsquo; absence as an automatic victory. Judgment in such circumstances is permissible only where the plaint raises no disputed question of fact: the pleadings must be verified, supported by affidavit, and the documents reliable. Finding those conditions met — the impugned material itself was on record — the Court held the averments stood admitted and that &ldquo;no disputed question of fact arises from the plaint which would warrant a trial.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>The substance: personality rights in the AI era</h2>
<p>On the merits, the Court anchored itself in the Delhi High Court&rsquo;s developing personality-rights jurisprudence protecting well-known individuals against misappropriation of name, likeness and voice, and found the harm here doubly serious: beyond the injury to the surgeon&rsquo;s reputation, members of the public might act on false medical claims — abandoning real treatment for a fabricated cure. The display of the hospital&rsquo;s registered marks in the videos separately made out infringement under Section 29 of the Trade Marks Act, 1999.</p>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Permanent injunction</h3><p>The interim order was made absolute against the posting defendants and the John Doe, restraining the impugned content.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Platform mechanism</h3><p>For future lookalike content, the plaintiffs may move Meta and Google directly on affidavit; the platforms must evaluate independently, with the court as backstop.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Reserved and dismissed</h3><p>The well-known-mark declaration was reserved for separate examination; damages and costs were dismissed as not pressed.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>For professionals and institutions, the case offers a realistic playbook against deepfake abuse: move fast for an ex parte injunction with platform and government defendants on the array; document public confusion; and seek a decree that includes a standing mechanism for future URLs, because deepfake campaigns respawn faster than fresh suits can be filed. For the public, it is a reminder that a familiar face in a medical video is no longer proof that the person ever said a word of it.</p>
</div>
